#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "035a4ded-7b92-5073-a8aa-3ffa47aff9ea" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Affine Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "91f986c6-c305-50eb-a5b7-36043279d128" :question/math-academy-id "e-9505" :question/problem "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_1$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_1$ can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u + v \\\\ v - 2u + 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u + v \\\\ -2u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_2$ can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ v - u\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ -u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\nIn fact, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation.\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_3$ is not affine since it contains the quadratic term $v^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{T}_1$ and $\\mathbf{T}_2$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd51cf5a-ca8a-5f89-91d6-8f1a58412314" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Representation of an Affine Transformation Given its Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9779a982-2048-58e2-8a90-df2bcb366d96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9506" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x = 5u + v - 6, \\qquad y = 2u + 4v$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5u + v - 6 \\\\ 2u + 4v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5u + v \\\\ 2u + 4v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+2+0 = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6664fc2f-f776-5da1-823a-c5ab35873e51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Representation of an Affine Transformation Given its Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "2ddc4e15-3729-5425-953c-1cb95edc201b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9508" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x = 3u-v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}, \\qquad y = u + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3u - v \\\\ u - 5v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3u - v + 1 \\\\ u - 5v\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 3u - v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{1}, \\qquad y = u + (\\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{-5})v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{0} \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-5)+0 = -4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "035a4ded-7b92-5073-a8aa-3ffa47aff9ea" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de2bb541-e52a-5f01-8123-740252417edc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168554} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee6fbd5-0131-512a-aafe-29aa94d6f4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168555} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd8680d-d5fa-535b-a007-2fc3cf82791c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168556} {:answer/id #uuid "38087fea-57af-5341-afc5-5c98be6982b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168557} {:answer/id #uuid "44f3c4af-7197-50a0-b127-0bb285d96b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38087fea-57af-5341-afc5-5c98be6982b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T_{1}$ and $T_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168557} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7c6755-b318-51f4-8a73-5e11986d7717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168553}] :question/id #uuid "4d581310-6942-54b8-b545-b203d0da1352" :question/math-academy-id "q-124007" :question/problem "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n1. $T_{1}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + v \\\\ v - u\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $T_{2}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} \\\\ -v\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T_{3}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + 2 \\\\ u - v + 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd51cf5a-ca8a-5f89-91d6-8f1a58412314" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Representation of an Affine Transformation Given its Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f09a5f2b-1ad2-54c2-91f8-f8cadcbb12b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168560} {:answer/id #uuid "166b26f4-c78c-560d-a59a-0e8177d66e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168561} {:answer/id #uuid "323ed335-fb2e-5758-84d3-20176fe394ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168562} {:answer/id #uuid "682a4471-d339-5ea0-87c1-699d262d1044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168563} {:answer/id #uuid "685a2114-6880-5ada-9ed5-303b4ca9325a" :answer/type {